<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hesiod</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hesiod was a Greek poet of around 700 BCE who lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascra">Ascra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where his father had settled after leaving Cyme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolis</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a> describes the origins of the gods and the world, and his Works and Days gives advice on farming, work and justice. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> he was regarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as a founder of their poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hesiod.jpg" alt="Hesiod" width="320" height="551" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yair Haklai · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Hesiod_(%3F)-Ancient_Roman_busts_in_the_Neues_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pindar of Thebes was the greatest choral lyric poet of the fifth century, Corinna of Tanagra was remembered as his rival, and the much earlier poet <b>Hesiod</b> had lived at Ascra below Mount Helicon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious and literary traditions absorbed from the Hattians and Hurrians were transmitted westward, and the Hurro-Hittite Kumarbi cycle, with its succession of warring sky gods, closely parallels <b>Hesiod</b>'s Theogony, one of the earliest documented channels of cultural transmission between the Near East and Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…from or were shaped by the older peoples of the Aegean and the civilisations of the Near East: <b>Hesiod</b>’s myth of the succession of the gods resembles Hurrian and Hittite myths of the kingship in heaven, and Aphrodite has much in common with the Near Eastern goddess Ishtar…</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>It continued with <b>Hesiod</b>’s poems on the gods and farming, and with the lyric poetry of Sappho, Alcaeus and Pindar.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Zeus, Hera, Poseidon, Hermes and Dionysus are all named on Linear B tablets of about 1200 BCE from Pylos, the oldest written records of the Greek gods, some five centuries before Homer and <b>Hesiod</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The ancients themselves placed the poet early: Herodotus, having consulted the Oracle at Dodona, put Homer and <b>Hesiod</b> about 400 years before his own time, which would be around 850 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cyme, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascra">Ascra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.76,26.94&amp;year=-700" aria-label="Cyme on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5218 773 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hesiod"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5668 1043h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5668 1043h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.76,26.94&amp;year=-700">What was here in 700 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Badness can be got easily and in shoals: the road to her is smooth, and she lives very near us. But between us and Goodness the gods have placed the sweat of our brows…&#8221;</p><footer>Works and Days &#183; tr. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/348/pg348-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Work is no disgrace: it is idleness which is a disgrace.&#8221;</p><footer>Works and Days &#183; tr. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/348/pg348-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…potter is angry with potter, and craftsman with craftsman, and beggar is jealous of beggar, and minstrel of minstrel.&#8221;</p><footer>Works and Days &#183; tr. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/348/pg348-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
